const intro={"n":26,"title":"Picardía Discovers He Is Sergeant Cruz's Son","nums":[957,959,962],"summary":"Called before the commander during the recruitment, Picardía hears himself described as a gambler, idler and prospective criminal like his father. The reference to his father awakens his curiosity, because he had not known who his father was. After investigating he learns that he is the son of Sergeant Cruz, who once left the police party to defend Martín Fierro. Picardía honors his father's memory and says that he has since reformed his behavior, although his nickname continues to follow him.","people":"Picardía, the first-person singer; the commander who questions him; the police officer who Picardía believes supplied the allegations; Sergeant Cruz, identified as his father.","themes":"Identity and recognition; fatherhood; reputation and stigma; personal reform; links between the poem's generations.","words":"antecesor (predecessor or ancestor in context, appearing in dialectal spelling); guapo (brave or formidable man); sargento (sergeant); enmienda (amendment or reform); buen nombre (good reputation).","questions":["What accusation about Picardía's father prompts him to investigate?","Whom does he identify as his father?","What episode in Cruz's life does Picardía recall?","How does the discovery affect his attitude to his own conduct?","Why does he say the name Picardía remains difficult to escape?"],"analysis":"The revelation should be attributed precisely to Picardía's testimony: he identifies Sergeant Cruz as his father and recalls the earlier rescue of Fierro. His assertion of reform does not erase the cheating he described in previous cantos; it creates an opportunity to compare his account of change with his inherited reputation.","related":"/en/students/vuelta-canto-6/","relatedTitle":"Cruz's final request concerning his son"};
